<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1995</h1><p class="ref-lede">1995 in world history: 13 events, 9 deaths, 1 state founded and 1 that came to an end. 190 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a><a href="/years/1992">1992</a><a href="/years/1993">1993</a><a href="/years/1994">1994</a><a href="/years/1995" aria-current="page">1995</a><a href="/years/1996">1996</a><a href="/years/1997">1997</a><a href="/years/1998">1998</a><a href="/years/1999">1999</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The World Trade Organization is established</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The WTO began work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, succeeding the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade of 1947. Unlike GATT, it covered services and intellectual property and had a binding system for settling trade disputes.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-17">17 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Great Hanshin earthquake devastates Kobe</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A magnitude 6.9 earthquake struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> region before dawn, killing 6,434 people and destroying over 100,000 buildings. The slow official response prompted reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s disaster planning.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Schengen Agreement abolishes border checks in Europe</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Seven European countries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, removed passport controls at their common borders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> now covers most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and several other states.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Truck bomb kills 168 in Oklahoma City</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Timothy McVeigh detonated a truck bomb outside the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building, killing 168 people, including 19 children. It remains the deadliest act of domestic terrorism in US history.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sampoong Department Store collapses in Seoul</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The five-storey store fell in seconds, killing 502 people. Investigators found illegal alterations and ignored warnings, prompting reforms of South Korean building safety.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Microsoft launches Windows 95</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Microsoft released Windows 95 with a huge marketing campaign; customers queued at midnight. Its Start menu and taskbar set the pattern of the personal computer desktop for decades.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Dayton Agreement is initialled, ending the Bosnian War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After three weeks of talks at an air base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> initialled a peace agreement. The war had killed about 100,000 people, including some 8,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Bill Clinton becomes the first US president to visit Northern Ireland</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Bill Clinton addressed crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> in support of the peace process. The visit added momentum to the talks that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> in 1998.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/cenepa-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cenepa-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/cenepa-war">Cenepa War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Cenepa War was a short conflict between Ecuador and Peru fought in January and February 1995 over outposts in the upper Cenepa valley, in an undemarcated stretch of the Cordillera del Cóndor.</p></div></li></ol></section>
